<header>
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Tamaclaude</p>
    <h1>Setting the panel up</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Twenty minutes, most of it one download. The panel needs your Mac —
      every frame is drawn there and sent over USB, which is why there is
      software to install at all.
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="shell">

  <h2><span class="n">00</span>Before you start</h2>
  <p>
    Two tools, neither of which macOS ships: <strong>Node 24</strong> and
    <strong>pnpm</strong>. If you use
    <a href="https://mise.jdx.dev">mise</a>, the project pins both and
    <code>mise install</code> will fetch them once you have the folder.
    Otherwise install Node 24 from
    <a href="https://nodejs.org">nodejs.org</a> and run
    <code>corepack enable pnpm</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing compiles on install, so you do not need Xcode — though you
    probably do need Apple's command line tools, since that is where
    <code>git</code> comes from. The first <code>git</code> command offers to
    install them.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="n">01</span>Get the project, and build it</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Clone it somewhere permanent</strong> — your home folder is fine,
    and it is what the rest of this page assumes. Two later steps record where
    it lives, so moving it afterwards stops the panel. If you do move it,
    re-run steps 05 and 06 from the new location and both re-point themselves.
  </p>
  <pre><code>cd ~
git clone https://github.com/Pushedskydiver/Tamaclaude.git
cd Tamaclaude
pnpm install
pnpm build</code></pre>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Everything below runs from inside that folder.</strong>
      <code>pnpm tamaclaude …</code> resolves against the nearest
      <code>package.json</code>, so it works there and in subfolders like
      <code>docs/</code>, and fails anywhere else — including the home folder
      a new Terminal window opens in. If a command ever answers
      <code>ERR_PNPM_NO_IMPORTER_MANIFEST_FOUND</code>, that is all it means:
      <code>cd ~/Tamaclaude</code> and try again.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    pnpm will warn that it ignored a build script. Leave it alone — nothing
    needed to run the panel builds anything. You can also skip the
    Playwright line in the README: that is for the test suite, and the
    animations are already drawn and baked into the code.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="n">02</span>Put your pack in place</h2>
  <p>
    A <em>pack</em> holds your colours, the things Clawd says, and the date he
    knows about. Yours was made for you and lives in its own small private
    repository — <strong>its address was sent to you separately</strong>, not
    printed here.
  </p>
  <pre><code>git clone &lt;your pack address&gt; ~/.tamaclaude/pack
pnpm tamaclaude pack</code></pre>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>It is a private repository, so git has to know who you are.</strong>
      Be signed in to GitHub on this Mac and have accepted the invitation
      first. If you see <code>Repository not found</code>, that is almost
      always access rather than a typo in the address — GitHub reports a
      private repo you cannot see and one that does not exist in exactly the
      same words.
    </p>
    <p>
      Handed a folder instead of an address? Copy its contents in, and mind
      the trailing slash — without it you get a folder inside a folder and
      the program will say it cannot find the pack:
    </p>
    <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/.tamaclaude/pack
cp -R /path/to/your-pack/ ~/.tamaclaude/pack/</code></pre>
  </div>
  <p>
    That last command should name your pack and say when its birthday fires.
    If it says <code>example</code>, or <code>birthday: none in this
    pack</code>, the wrong folder is in place — worth stopping for, because
    the panel will look completely correct and quietly do the wrong thing on
    the one day it matters.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="n">03</span>Plug the panel in</h2>
  <p>
    Use the USB cable, into the Mac itself rather than a hub if you have the
    choice. You should see the boot screen: Clawd waving next to the name.
    Do this before the next step — the program finds the panel by looking for
    it, so it needs to already be there.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="n">04</span>Start it, then stop it</h2>
  <pre><code>pnpm tamaclaude daemon</code></pre>
  <p>
    The boot screen should be replaced by Clawd beside his rock pool. Then
    press <code>Ctrl-C</code> to stop it. It will print
    <code>ELIFECYCLE Command failed</code> on the way out — that is what
    stopping looks like, not a problem. The next step starts the same program
    automatically and two copies cannot both hold the panel.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="n">05</span>Make it start when you log in</h2>
  <p>This one prints what it would do and changes nothing:</p>
  <pre><code>pnpm tamaclaude install-agent</code></pre>
  <p>When it looks right:</p>
  <pre><code>pnpm tamaclaude install-agent --apply
pnpm tamaclaude status</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>--apply</code> should end with <code>Installed and running</code>,
    and <code>status</code> should say <code>agent running (pid …)</code>. If
    you get <code>agent loaded but not running; last exit 1</code>, a
    <code>pnpm tamaclaude daemon</code> from step 04 is still running in
    another Terminal window and is holding the panel — close it, then re-run
    the <code>--apply</code> line.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="n">06</span>Connect it to Claude Code</h2>
  <p>Prints the change and writes nothing:</p>
  <pre><code>pnpm tamaclaude-install-hooks</code></pre>
  <p>When it looks right:</p>
  <pre><code>pnpm tamaclaude-install-hooks --apply</code></pre>
  <p>
    This edits <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>, which is where your own
    Claude Code settings live, so it is worth reading what it says it will add
    before you let it.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Start a new Claude Code session before you test.</strong> Hooks
      are read when a session starts, so one that was already open knows
      nothing about them — and the hook is deliberately silent when anything
      goes wrong, so an already-open session shows you exactly what a failed
      install would. It has not failed; it just is not listening yet.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then ask Claude Code to <strong>read</strong> a file and watch — Clawd
    should start climbing. Opening a session is not enough; that puts him at
    rest. Reading rather than editing, because reading needs no approval, and
    a first edit tends to land on the permission screen rather than the typing
    you were watching for.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>When it stops</h2>
  <p>Start here:</p>
  <pre><code>cd ~/Tamaclaude        # or wherever you put it
pnpm tamaclaude status</code></pre>
  <p>Three things account for almost everything.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Clawd stopped reacting, but the panel is still on.</strong> The
      Claude Code wiring stores the full path to Node, and it is deliberately
      silent when that path breaks — it must never interrupt a session, so a
      failure prints nothing anywhere and <code>status</code> still looks
      fine. This is the most likely thing to happen after a Node upgrade.
      Re-point it with
      <code>pnpm tamaclaude-install-hooks --apply</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>Stuck on the boot screen, or never came back after a
    restart.</strong> Usually the login entry pointing at a Node that moved.
    <code>pnpm tamaclaude status</code> says so, and
    <code>pnpm tamaclaude install-agent --apply</code> re-points it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>You started it by hand and forgot.</strong> A
    <code>pnpm tamaclaude daemon</code> still running in a Terminal window
    holds the panel, so the automatic one cannot. Close that window; the
    automatic one takes over within a minute.
  </p>
  <p>
    If none of those, the log is at
    <code>~/.tamaclaude/daemon.log</code> — written only by the automatic
    startup, so it will not exist if you never got that far. And note that
    <code>status</code> reports the program, not the hardware: to check the
    panel is found, run <code>pnpm tamaclaude install-agent</code>, which
    prints the device it would use.
  </p>

  <h2>Changing your pack later</h2>
  <p>
    From the project folder — <code>git -C</code> pulls the pack without
    moving you, which matters because the two lines after it only run here:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git -C ~/.tamaclaude/pack pull
pnpm tamaclaude uninstall-agent
pnpm tamaclaude install-agent --apply</code></pre>
  <p>
    The program reads the pack once when it starts, so it has to start again.
    Unplugging the panel is not enough. Logging out and back in does it too,
    if you would rather not type anything.
  </p>

